      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Definite Integrals",
      "problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{2}{3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/2$ | ${e}^{2}/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{2}{3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
